<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Republic of Paraguay</h1><p class="st-meta">1811 CE – present · capital Asunción</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A landlocked South American republic shaped by long dictatorships and one of history's most devastating wars, and among the most bilingual nations in the world.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1811 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a> defeated an army from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-belgrano">Manuel Belgrano</a> and in May overthrew the Spanish governor of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/governorate-of-paraguay">Governorate of Paraguay</a>, becoming independent. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-gaspar-rodriguez-de-francia">José Gaspar Rodríguez de Francia</a> ruled as dictator from 1814, and as perpetual dictator from 1816 until his death in 1840, closing the country to outside contact and confiscating church lands.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlos-antonio-lopez">Carlos Antonio López</a> opened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a> and built railways and an ironworks. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-solano-lopez">Francisco Solano López</a> led it into the War of the Triple Alliance against <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/new-republic-brazil">Brazil</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/argentine-republic">Argentina</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/oriental-republic-of-uruguay">Uruguay</a> from 1864 to 1870, in which Paraguay lost much of its population and territory. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaco-war">Chaco War</a> of 1932 to 1935 it defeated the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-bolivia">Republic of Bolivia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfredo-stroessner">Alfredo Stroessner</a> ruled from 1954 to 1989.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfredo-stroessner">Stroessner</a>'s overthrow in 1989, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a> adopted a democratic constitution in 1992. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/itaipu-dam">Itaipú</a> dam, shared with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/new-republic-brazil">Brazil</a>, made it a major exporter of electricity, alongside soya and beef. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guarani-language">Guaraní</a> and Spanish are both official languages.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/republic-of-paraguay.jpg" alt="Republic of Paraguay" width="320" height="176"><figcaption>Image: Republica del Paraguay · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Paraguay.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1811 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Asunción</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>José Gaspar Rodríguez de Francia, Francisco Solano López, Alfredo Stroessner</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish, Guaraní</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/paraguay">Paraguay</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Paraguay, República del Paraguay</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1918&amp;focus=republic-of-paraguay" aria-label="Republic of Paraguay on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3153 3107 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Republic of Paraguay lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3473 3363L3473 3363M3473 3363L3517 3686"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3473 3363h0M3517 3686h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3473 3363h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3473 3363h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Asunción. <a href="/atlas?year=1918&amp;focus=republic-of-paraguay">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
